WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.962
A pre-climb inspection needs a clear sequence and a record of what happened.

00:00:06.704 --> 00:00:10.943
Safe2Climb guides the inspector through the pole, attachments, setting,

00:00:11.229 --> 00:00:12.859
movement and base condition.

00:00:13.797 --> 00:00:17.560
Each step keeps observations connected to the inspection, making the

00:00:17.574 --> 00:00:19.232
process easier to review.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.304
The U B one thousand check adds another layer of inspection evidence.

00:00:27.046 --> 00:00:29.071
It supplements the physical checks.

00:00:30.008 --> 00:00:34.071
The prototype demonstrates acquisition and follow-up states with example

00:00:34.084 --> 00:00:34.682
results.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:39.329
Those examples are not live measurements, and they do not replace an

00:00:39.342 --> 00:00:41.462
approved climbing decision procedure.

00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:47.341
A lean record can retain a photograph and an indicated pole alignment.

00:00:49.083 --> 00:00:52.453
Movement observations can include a recorded amount and a note.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:57.480
Together, these records make the observation clearer for another person.

00:00:58.418 --> 00:01:02.113
This prototype uses an image angle and manually entered movement.

00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:09.128
When something causes concern, record the reason and supporting evidence.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:14.905
Inspection history keeps issues visible for review and handover, with

00:01:14.919 --> 00:01:16.875
the pole and inspection context.

00:01:17.813 --> 00:01:19.892
That creates an auditable record.

00:01:20.829 --> 00:01:25.095
This local prototype demonstrates the workflow; it does not send a live

00:01:25.109 --> 00:01:26.604
network notification.
